==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow||*pro-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|prōsecūtus}}, perfect participle of {{m|la|prōsequor}}. {{doublet|en|pursue}}, from Old French. Compare also {{m|en|persecute}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɒsɪkjuːt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-prosecute.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|law}} To [[start]] [[criminal]] [[proceeding]]s against.
#: {{ux|en|to '''prosecute''' a man for trespass, or for a riot}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Samson|line=898|page=56|passage=To acquit themſelves and '''proſecute''' their foes}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|law}} To [[charge]], [[try]].
#* {{RQ:William Burroughs Naked Lunch|page=8|passage=The Vigilante is '''prosecuted''' in Federal Court under a lynch bill and winds up in a Federal Nut House specially designed for the containment of ghosts {{...}}}}
# To seek to obtain by legal process.
#: {{ux|en|to '''prosecute''' a right or a claim in a court of law}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pursue]] something to the [[end]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''prosecute''' a scheme, hope, or an investigation}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=I|scene=i|passage=I am beloved of beauteous Hermia; / Why should not I, then, '''prosecute''' my right?}}
#* {{quote-web|1=en|date=2022-10-22|work=w:Ministry of Home Affairs (Singapore)|title=Ministry of Home Affairs' Response to Sir Richard Branson's Blog Post on 10 October 2022|url=https://www.mha.gov.sg/mediaroom/press-releases/ministry-of-home-affairs-response-to-sir-richard-branson-blog-post-on-10-october-2022/|text=Nor do we believe that a country that '''prosecuted''' [[Opium War|two wars in China in the 19th century]] to force the Chinese to accept opium imports has any moral right to lecture Asians on drugs.}}

